WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.767 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Does the decimal with more digits always have a greater
value?

00:00:04.417 --> 00:00:07.760 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today we are comparing and ordering decimals.

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:13.853 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We will line up equal places, use placeholder zeros, and
find the first place where the values differ.

00:00:14.073 --> 00:00:19.166 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Which would you predict is larger: four point six eight or
four point six zero seven?

00:00:19.816 --> 00:00:26.416 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our learning goal is to compare, order and locate decimal
numbers using place value and number lines.

00:00:27.066 --> 00:00:28.823 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Success criteria.

00:00:29.043 --> 00:00:32.164 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can compare decimal digits from left to right.

00:00:32.384 --> 00:00:36.654 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can use placeholder zeros to compare equivalent decimal
forms.

00:00:36.874 --> 00:00:41.686 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can locate decimals accurately between labelled points on
a number line.

00:00:42.336 --> 00:00:44.276 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Compare from left to right.

00:00:44.496 --> 00:00:49.253 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
If the whole numbers match, compare tenths, then hundredths,
then thousandths.

00:00:49.473 --> 00:00:53.601 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Trailing zeros can show equal places without changing a
value.

00:00:53.821 --> 00:00:56.877 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Decimal digits are not a separate whole number.

00:00:57.527 --> 00:01:01.531 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Compare four point six eight and four point six zero seven.

00:01:01.751 --> 00:01:05.475 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Rename four point six eight as four point six eight zero.

00:01:05.694 --> 00:01:07.722 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The ones and tenths match.

00:01:08.372 --> 00:01:11.614 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
At the hundredths place, eight is greater than zero.

00:01:11.834 --> 00:01:15.744 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
That decides the comparison: four point six eight is
greater.

00:01:15.964 --> 00:01:18.358 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The thousandths cannot reverse it.

00:01:19.008 --> 00:01:23.339 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Now compare zero point six two with zero point five nine
nine.

00:01:23.559 --> 00:01:25.640 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Both have zero whole ones.

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:29.028 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
At the tenths place, six is greater than five.

00:01:29.678 --> 00:01:34.718 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
So zero point six two is greater, even though it has fewer
written decimal digits.

00:01:34.938 --> 00:01:40.019 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Comparing sixty-two with five hundred and ninety-nine would
compare the wrong units.

00:01:40.669 --> 00:01:44.888 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Which is greater: two point four zero five, or two point
four five?

00:01:48.888 --> 00:01:52.342 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Two point four five equals two point four five zero.

00:01:52.562 --> 00:01:57.145 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The first differing place is the hundredths, so two point
four five is greater.

00:01:57.795 --> 00:02:01.946 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Align equal place values and compare from the largest place.

00:02:02.166 --> 00:02:04.121 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Stop at the first difference.

00:02:04.341 --> 00:02:10.196 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
On a number line the greater number is further right; the
number of written digits alone does not decide.
